{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-36342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-36342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {e}^{-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} & =c \\\\ -{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡c \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $0<c\\le 1$ since $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c$ must be non-negative. If we let $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c={r}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surfaces form a family of spheres centered at the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-36342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "25202b02c58253dd349657c72e1ea1dd909de088ce40ffba57ffc6d14b895a6b"} {"question" "q-36342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-36342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A family of spheres centered at $(0,0,e)$"] ["text" "A family of spheres centered at $(0,0,{e}^{-1})$"] ["text" "A family of spheres centered at the origin"] ["text" "A family of surfaces given by  ${z}^{2}+c={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $c>0$"] ["text" "A family of surfaces given by ${z}^{2}+c={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $c\\ne 0$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-36342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A family of spheres centered at the origin"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-37131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z$ that passes through the point $P(0,1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-37131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1,1) & =0\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ xy+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-xy \\\\ z & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-xy) \\\\ z & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(0,1,1)$ is the surface $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-37131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "25202b02c58253dd349657c72e1ea1dd909de088ce40ffba57ffc6d14b895a6b"} {"question" "q-37131" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-37131" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy"] ["math" "z=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy"] ["math" "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy"] ["math" "z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy"] ["math" "z=\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-37131" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-71574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z)=z-x-y-1,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-71574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z-x-y-1 & =1 \\\\ z & =x+y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the plane $z=x+y+2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-71574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "25202b02c58253dd349657c72e1ea1dd909de088ce40ffba57ffc6d14b895a6b"} {"question" "q-71574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-71574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=-x-y-1"] ["math" "z=\\frac{1}{x+y-1}"] ["math" "z=x+y+1"] ["math" "z=x+y+2"] ["math" "z=x-y+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-71574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "z=x+y+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-71593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{xy}$ that passes through the point $P(1,1,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-71593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,2) & =\\frac{(2)-(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}{(1)(1)}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{z-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{xy} & =2 \\\\ z-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2xy \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(1,1,2)$ is the surface $z={x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-71593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "25202b02c58253dd349657c72e1ea1dd909de088ce40ffba57ffc6d14b895a6b"} {"question" "q-71593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-71593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=(x+y)^{2}"] ["math" "z=(x-y)^{2}"] ["math" "z={x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}"] ["math" "z={x}^{2}-2xy-{y}^{2}"] ["math" "z={y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-71593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "z={x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-129199" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-z-3}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-129199" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {e}^{4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-z-3} & =c \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-z-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡c \\\\ z & =4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c>0.$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of elliptic paraboloids:\n\n$$\nz=4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c,\\,c\\in (0,\\infty )\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-129199" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "25202b02c58253dd349657c72e1ea1dd909de088ce40ffba57ffc6d14b895a6b"} {"question" "q-129199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-129199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z=-4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}+3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡c$"] ["text" "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z=4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c$"] ["text" "A family of hyperboloids of two sheets: ${z}^{2}=4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c$"] ["text" "A family of surfaces: $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-3)+c$"] ["text" "A family of surfaces: $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-3-c)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"} {"question" "q-129199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z=4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068037/state.json" "file_sha256" "48c6e132fb59be6ec9fb5fbf7770c113a1b25d348eb003196cf92b8bd0b61caa"}] "usage" {"q-36342" []} "basis" 2288 "retractions" [[17592186129536 :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x, y, z) = e^{-x^{2} - y^{2} - z^{2}}$?"] [17592186129536 :question/answer-fields 17592186127010]] "immutable_answers" [17592186127011 17592186127012 17592186127013 17592186127014 17592186127015] "immutable_fields" [[17592186127010 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127010 :answer-field/id] [17592186127010 :answer-field/key] [17592186127010 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
